  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
    "intro": "$\\sqrt{10}$ is the side length of a square of area $10$. Because $10$ sits between the perfect squares $9$ and $16$, $\\sqrt{10}$ must sit between $3$ and $4$.",
    "facts": [
      "If $a^{2}<x<b^{2}$, then $a<\\sqrt{x}<b$.",
      "If $a^{3}<x<b^{3}$, then $a<\\sqrt[3]{x}<b$.",
      "The landmarks are the perfect squares $1,4,9,16,25,36,49,64,81,100,\\ldots$ and the perfect cubes $1,8,27,64,125,216,343,\\ldots$",
      "The root sits nearer the landmark the number is nearer to: $50$ is much nearer $49$ than $64$, so $\\sqrt{50}$ is a little above $7$.",
      "An estimate is not exact, so record a decimal value with $\\approx$, for example $\\sqrt{10}\\approx 3.16$."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Find the two perfect squares (or cubes) the number lies between.",
      "Take the root of each landmark to get the two whole numbers.",
      "Decide which of the two whole numbers the root is closer to."
    ]
  },
